= Maternidad voluntaria

The feminist movement has found its unignorable demand, and it is the one that makes the whole Mexican political class flinch in unison: *maternidad voluntaria* — voluntary motherhood, which in plain speech means the decriminalization of abortion, in a Catholic country where the word cannot be said on the radio. The Coalición de Mujeres has put it on the table; the FNALIDM is forming around it; and the question travels, inevitably, to your door, because the Party is one of the very few organizations in the Republic that could support it and survive.

The calculation is brutal and clarifying. Backing it costs you the Catholic campesino whose land you were courting and hands the regime's press a gift it will not decline. Ducking it tells the movement's most committed women exactly where they rank. There is no straddle; the demand was designed, by people who understand the Left, to have no straddle.

- @respaldar_maternidad: Support it, in the program and in the Chamber if you have a tribune. The Party stands with the demand no one else will say aloud.
- @apoyo_tibio: A careful position: support "women's right to decide" in principle, decline the legislative fight, keep the campesino and the feminist both half-satisfied.
- @rechazar_maternidad: Decline. The class base is Catholic and rural; this is a fight for another decade, fought by other people.

@respaldar_maternidad
choose-if: feminismo >= 20
unavailable-subtitle: Backing an unbackable demand requires a women's movement already inside the house, vouching for the risk.
on-arrival: feminismo += 20; sup_students += 4; sup_middle += 3; sup_peasants -= 3; legitimidad_regimen -= 2; expediente += 2; fac_ortodoxos -= 2

The Party does the thing almost no one in Mexican public life will do in 1979: it says the word, in its program and, if it holds a tribune, on the floor of the Chamber, where a deputy of yours reads the case for voluntary motherhood into a record the bishops cannot expunge. The cost is immediate and real — the rural Catholic base recoils, the regime's press discovers a new adjective for you, the ortodoxos mutter about priorities. And the gain is the kind that compounds: the feminist movement, which trusts no party, learns it can trust this one on the demand that mattered most, and a generation of organized women decide the Left has, at last, a house with their name on a room. Decades early, the Party is on the right side of the argument the country will eventually have.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@apoyo_tibio
on-arrival: feminismo += 6; sup_middle += 1; unity += 1; des_nueva += 3

The position is a masterpiece of not quite: the Party affirms "the right of women to decide over their own bodies" in a resolution carefully unaccompanied by any bill, any vote, any risk. It satisfies the memo and no one else. The feminists read it exactly right — as the sound a party makes when it wants the credit without the cost — and bank it as one more reason to build their movement at arm's length. The campesino, who was never going to read the resolution, is unmoved. You have spent the issue and bought nothing with it, which is the specific talent of the careful position.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@rechazar_maternidad
on-arrival: feminismo -= 4; sup_peasants += 2; fac_ortodoxos += 2; des_nueva += 4

The decision is made on the arithmetic of the base, and the arithmetic is not wrong: the rural Catholic vote is real and the feminist vote is small and the Party has a reform to protect. It is also the decision that tells the movement's women, permanently, that the Left will spend them the moment they cost anything — and they remember, the way the excluded always remember, and build accordingly. The abortion fight will be won in Mexico eventually, by a movement that learned in exactly these years to expect nothing from the parties, including yours.

- @post_event: Continuar.

