= El Frente de mujeres

The scattered groups converge, the way the Left's best energies always eventually try to: the Coalición, the feminist collectives, the union women, the colonia women, the party militants who kept a foot in both worlds — into the Frente Nacional de Lucha por la Liberación y los Derechos de las Mujeres, a coalition broad enough to frighten its own members with its potential. It is the women's movement becoming a political force instead of a moral one, and it arrives at your door not asking permission this time but offering an alliance between equals — which is, for a party that spent forty years addressing "the woman comrade" in the singular, a novel and slightly vertiginous proposition.

- @aliar_fnalidm: Ally as equals — the Party and the Frente as partners, campaigns run jointly, the women's agenda a plank and not a footnote.
- @cortejar_fnalidm: Court it for the electoral machine — welcome the alliance, quietly hope to absorb its energy into the Party's own campaigns.

@aliar_fnalidm
choose-if: feminismo >= 40
unavailable-subtitle: An alliance between equals requires the Party to have actually earned the movement's trust — feminismo it has not yet built.
on-arrival: feminismo += 15; frente_unidad += 3; sup_middle += 3; sup_students += 3; fe_voto += 2; political_capital += 2

The alliance is signed as what it is — a meeting of two forces, not the annexation of one by the other — and it holds because the Party, for once, resists the reflex to swallow what it joins. The Frente's campaigns and the Party's move in tandem: the colonias organized around water and childcare in the same breath, the union women and the feminist students discovering they were always the same fight seen from two windows. When 1988 comes, the women's movement is inside the convergence as a founder, and the wave is larger and more durable for carrying, at last, the half of the country the Left spent its history addressing in the singular.

- @post_event: Continuar.

@cortejar_fnalidm
on-arrival: feminismo += 4; frente_unidad += 1; sup_middle += 1; des_nueva += 3

You welcome the Frente warmly and set about, with the old instinct, folding its energy into the Party's own machine — its activists deployed to your campaigns, its demands translated into your language, its autonomy gently discouraged. The feminists, who have watched every party on the Left try exactly this, decline to be absorbed with a courtesy that is its own verdict. The alliance is real but shallow, useful for a season and mistrusted for a decade, and the lesson the movement takes is the one it keeps relearning: that even the best of the parties would rather have women as a resource than as a partner.

- @post_event: Continuar.
